Step 1: Start MongoDB using CMD
CMD 1 — MongoDB Server
Open Command Prompt and type:
mongod


Expected result: The MongoDB server starts, normally listening on port 27017.
Keep this CMD window open.

CMD 2 — MongoDB Shell
Open another Command Prompt and type:
mongo


Expected result:
MongoDB shell version ...
connecting to: mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/
>


If your installed version uses the newer MongoDB Shell, enter mongosh instead of mongo.

Enter all the following MongoDB commands in CMD 2.

Part A: Create and drop a database
Step 2 — Display existing databases
show dbs


Sample output:
admin    0.000GB
config   0.000GB
local    0.000GB


Step 3 — Create/select a database
use Romal


Output:
switched to db Romal


The database is selected, but MongoDB does not persist it until you store data.
Step 4 — Create a collection to save the database
db.createCollection("Practical1")


Output:
{ "ok" : 1 }


Step 5 — Display databases and collections
show dbs
show collections


Expected collection output:
Practical1


Step 6 — Drop the database
db.dropDatabase()


Output:
{ "ok" : 1 }



Part B: Create, display and drop a collection
Step 7 — Select the database again
use Romal


Step 8 — Create a collection
db.createCollection("Practical1")


Output:
{ "ok" : 1 }


Step 9 — Display collections
show collections


Output:
Practical1


Step 10 — Drop the collection
db.Practical1.drop()


Output:
true







Part C: Insert, query, update and delete a document
Step 11 — Select the database
use Romal


Step 12 — Insert a document
db.Doc.insertOne({Name:"Romal",Age:10})


Output:
{
  "acknowledged": true,
  "insertedId": ObjectId("...")
}


Step 13 — Display the document
db.Doc.find()


Expected output (your generated _id will differ):
{
  "_id": ObjectId("..."),
  "Name": "Romal",
  "Age": 10
}


Step 14 — Update the document
db.Doc.updateOne(
  {Name:"Romal"},
  {$set:{Age:20}}
)


Output:
{
  "acknowledged": true,
  "matchedCount": 1,
  "modifiedCount": 1
}


Verify:
db.Doc.find()


Expected result:
{
  "_id": ObjectId("..."),
  "Name": "Romal",
  "Age": 20
}


Step 15 — Delete the document
db.Doc.deleteOne({Name:"Romal"})


Output:
{
  "acknowledged": true,
  "deletedCount": 1
}


Verify:
db.Doc.find()


Output: No documents returned.

Part D: Employee queries
Your journal contains six Employee tasks, including inserting documents and filtering employees by city, salary and department.
Step 16 — Create Employee records
Use the following sample dataset, consistent with the journal's queries. The names and fields are based on the journal, with values organized here so you can execute every query in sequence.
use Employee


db.col.insertMany([
  {Name:"Smita",Salary:100000,Dept:"IT",City:"Mumbai"},
  {Name:"Romal",Salary:15000,Dept:"IT",City:"Thane"},
  {Name:"Sujal",Salary:9000,Dept:"Tester",City:"Bandra"},
  {Name:"Manav",Salary:30000,Dept:"Finance",City:"Kurla"},
  {Name:"Hansa",Salary:20000,Dept:"Developer",City:"Pune"}
])


Output:
{
  "acknowledged": true,
  "insertedIds": {
    "0": ObjectId("..."),
    "1": ObjectId("..."),
    "2": ObjectId("..."),
    "3": ObjectId("..."),
    "4": ObjectId("...")
  }
}


Step 17 — Display all Employee records
db.col.find()


Output: All five Employee documents, including their generated _id values.
Step 18 — Display employees living in Mumbai
db.col.find({City:"Mumbai"})


Expected matching record:
Smita | 100000 | IT | Mumbai


Step 19 — Display employees living in Kurla with salary greater than 19000
db.col.find({
  City:"Kurla",
  Salary:{$gt:19000}
})


Expected matching record:
Manav | 30000 | Finance | Kurla


Step 20 — Display employees whose salary is greater than or equal to 10000 but less than 20000
db.col.find({
  Salary:{$gte:10000,$lt:20000}
})


Expected matching record:
Romal | 15000 | IT | Thane


Step 21 — Display employees living in Thane or Mumbai with salary greater than 10000
db.col.find({
  $or:[
    {City:"Thane"},
    {City:"Mumbai"}
  ],
  Salary:{$gt:10000}
})


Expected matching records:
Smita | 100000 | IT | Mumbai
Romal | 15000  | IT | Thane


Step 22 — Display employees whose salary is not equal to 10000 and whose department is Developer or Tester
db.col.find({
  Salary:{$ne:10000},
  $or:[
    {Dept:"Developer"},
    {Dept:"Tester"}
  ]
})


Expected matching records:
Sujal | 9000  | Tester    | Bandra
Hansa | 20000 | Developer | Pune
